            <ArticleTitle>Management of thromboembolism-in-transit with pulmonary embolism.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">ERP-17-0043</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1530/ERP-17-0043</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present a rare complication of deep venous thrombosis with pulmonary embolism that threatened the patient with systemic embolization. A 36-year-old female was referred to the hospital after five days of progressive shortness of breath and chest pain. Preceding onset of symptoms she had undergone surgery leading to reduced physical activity and had just returned from vacation by a long flight. Investigations with transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography revealed a thromboembolism-in-transit across a patent foramen ovale. Thoracic CT showed submassive bilateral pulmonary embolism. Hemodynamic parameters were stable. The patient was treated surgically with extraction of the thrombus, closure of the foramen ovale and removal of the bilateral pulmonary emboli. She was discharged after an uneventful hospital stay.</AbstractText>
